#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082980 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "332fefd8-7de7-5a84-81e2-a2aa97be9b73" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Expected Value for a Random Variable with Uniform Probability"}] :question/id #uuid "27a80de6-49d0-557b-8444-ebab046e795b" :question/math-academy-id "e-6074" :question/problem "What is the expected value of one roll of a tetrahedral die?" :question/worked-solution "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} x \\cdot f(x)\n$$\n\nA tetrahedral die has four sides. Let $X$ be the score attained on a random roll of the die. Then the support is\n\n$$\nS = \\{1,2,3,4 \\}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding probabilities are all\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\nf(1) = f(2) = f(3) = f(4) = \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= 1 \\cdot f(1) + 2 \\cdot f(2) + 3 \\cdot f(3) + 4 \\cdot f(4) \\\\\n&= 1(\\frac{1}{4}) + 2(\\frac{1}{4}) + 3(\\frac{1}{4}) + 4(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{2}{4} + \\frac{3}{4} + \\frac{4}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 + 2 + 3 + 4}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{10}{4} \\\\\n&= 2.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082981 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90d0c882-c94b-5759-9a78-63979365e318" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Expected Profit of a Game"}] :question/id #uuid "2b946455-893f-57db-b0a5-d52091914cf2" :question/math-academy-id "e-6075" :question/problem "Suppose that we play a game with a stack of five cards labeled $1$ through $5$. A card is drawn at random. Drawing $1,2,3$, or $4$ means that you lose $\\[math]1$, and drawing a $5$ means that you win $\\[/math] 5$. What is the expected profit at each drawing in this game?" :question/worked-solution "Let $p$ represent the profit on a draw in this game. On each draw in the game, there are two possible outcomes:\n\n- drawing $1,2,3,4$, the player loses $\\[math]1$, so we have $p=-1$.\n- drawing $5$, the player wins $\\[/math]5$, so we have $p=5$.\n\nThe probabilities for the outcomes are\n\n$$\nP(-1)=\\dfrac{4}{5}, \\qquad P(5)=\\dfrac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nLet's summarize this in a table. If $p$ is the profit (in dollars) on a single draw, and $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p$, then we have the following:\n\n| $p$ | $-1$ | $5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(p)$ | $\\dfrac{4}{5}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\textrm E[p] &= -1 \\cdot f(-1) + 5 \\cdot f(5) \\\\[5pt] &= -1 \\left(\\dfrac{4}{5} \\right) + 5 \\left(\\dfrac{1}{5} \\right) \\\\[5pt] &=-\\dfrac{4}{5} + 1 \\\\[3pt] &=\\dfrac{1}{5} \\\\[3pt] &=0.2 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit at each draw in this game is $\\[math]0.20$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083021 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8297713-fb86-59f2-af4d-850af2575541" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Expected Value for a Random Variable with Non-Uniform Probability"}] :question/id #uuid "e6dd60de-5df1-531d-9971-847343303fd7" :question/math-academy-id "e-7383" :question/problem "The rolls of an unfair tetrahedral die follow the probability mass function $f(x)$ as shown in the table below. What is the expected value of one roll of the die?\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\dfrac{1}{10}$ | $\\dfrac{3}{10}$ | $\\dfrac{2}{5}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ |" :question/worked-solution "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} x \\cdot f(x)\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= 1 \\cdot f(1) + 2 \\cdot f(2) + 3 \\cdot f(3) + 4 \\cdot f(4) \\\\\n&= 1(\\frac{1}{10}) + 2(\\frac{3}{10}) + 3(\\frac{2}{5}) + 4(\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{10} + \\frac{6}{10} + \\frac{6}{5} + \\frac{4}{5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{10} + \\frac{6}{10} + \\frac{12}{10} + \\frac{8}{10} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 + 6 + 12 + 8}{10} \\\\\n&= \\frac{27}{10} \\\\\n&= 2.7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083022 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c3fc090-22e5-5f05-9634-b8c645cf5700" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Expected Value Given the Frequency of a Random Variable"}] :question/id #uuid "ed80b546-956c-5b67-ac7d-0c0049c2278c" :question/math-academy-id "e-7385" :question/problem "Several children were asked about how many pets they have. The results were recorded below. If $X$ is the number of pets that a randomly selected child has, what is the expected value of $X$?\n\n| Number of pets | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Number of Children | $5$ | $6$ | $5$ |" :question/worked-solution "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} x \\cdot f(x)\n$$\n\nWe're given the frequencies of the number of pets, so we can start by computing the corresponding probabilities. The total number of children is\n\n$$\n5+6+5=16\n$$\n\nso the corresponding probabilities are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X = 0) = f(0) &= \\frac{5}{16} \\\\\nP(X = 1) = f(1) &= \\frac{6}{16} = \\frac{3}{8} \\\\\nP(X = 2) = f(2) &= \\frac{5}{16}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe organize these results in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\dfrac{5}{16}$ | $\\dfrac{3}{8}$ | $\\dfrac{5}{16}$ |\n\nNow, summing up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= 0 \\cdot f(0) + 1 \\cdot f(1) + 2 \\cdot f(2) \\\\\n&= 0(\\frac{5}{16}) + 1(\\frac{3}{8}) + 2(\\frac{5}{16}) \\\\\n&= 0 + \\frac{3}{8} + \\frac{10}{16} \\\\\n&= 0 + \\frac{6}{16} + \\frac{10}{16} \\\\\n&= \\frac{0 + 6 + 10}{16} \\\\\n&= \\frac{16}{16} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083614 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8297713-fb86-59f2-af4d-850af2575541" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Expected Value for a Random Variable with Non-Uniform Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b34cd313-fd62-5ad4-b618-2a42a66d01be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081051} {:answer/id #uuid "40e89c05-8d93-5372-8229-43947f491366" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081052} {:answer/id #uuid "4596cb71-ec73-5fd0-a2c7-e18ebadafedb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081053} {:answer/id #uuid "6129c28a-6d6f-5675-bc1b-45a1f8ee1d9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081054} {:answer/id #uuid "66d01d8d-f2b2-504d-aaaa-d69613e606a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081055}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6129c28a-6d6f-5675-bc1b-45a1f8ee1d9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081054} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1cf4234-f230-55e1-9fe1-da3206be967e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081050}] :question/id #uuid "9734bee1-71b3-5340-a6c8-32c60363a7cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-42728" :question/problem "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nThe rolls of an unfair six-sided die follow the probability mass function $f(x)$ as shown in the table below. What is the expected value of one roll of the die?\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $